#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/id #uuid "9815dede-5c47-5430-a58a-218313f78412" :question/math-academy-id "e-10014" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-2x^2 - 4x = 1.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $x^2 + 2x + 1$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\nx^2 + 2x + 1 = (x + 1)^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-4x & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2x) & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x) & =1 \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =1 \\\\ -2[(x+1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ -2[(x+1)^{2}-1] & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+(-2)(-1) & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+2 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $2$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-2),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(x+1)^{2}+2 & =1 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2}+2-2 & =1-2 \\\\ -2(x+1)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-2)} \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x+1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |x+1| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184083 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "6e12005d-4652-532c-a715-3ab9d9ad3618" :question/math-academy-id "e-7139" :question/problem "Write the equation $2x^2 - 6x = 1$ in the form $(x+p)^2 = q,$ and hence solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide out by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-6x & =1 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-3x) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\dfrac32$ and $q=\\dfrac{11}{4}.$ Now, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{11}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184084 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "9dfe0c3a-2c54-5270-ab0d-0d2dad09a199" :question/math-academy-id "e-7140" :question/problem "Write the equation $-2x^2+2x+1 = 0$ in the form $2(x+p)^2=q,$ and hence solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-2{x}^{2}+2x+1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =1 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-x) & =1 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =1 \\\\ 2[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =1 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ 2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\dfrac12$ and $q=\\dfrac32.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51235c4b-9ea8-5be6-9202-8faae512d7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $z=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399769} {:answer/id #uuid "18c50e74-54e2-5b08-8781-bdac4e3eb829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399770} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef860dc-5a1c-5a5d-8cba-4a4b862a9b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399771} {:answer/id #uuid "c6502671-64f9-549c-ad24-6af57af49c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=9,\\,z=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $z=\\frac{9}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399772} {:answer/id #uuid "605403ec-6766-5b0b-898a-15f1f1697f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "605403ec-6766-5b0b-898a-15f1f1697f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=2,\\,z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399773} :answer-field/id #uuid "132377e4-a98b-5f45-bb48-4f5451b52524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b0945056-0521-55d9-80fa-5db83ff53407" :question/math-academy-id "q-112612" :question/problem "The equation $-4{z}^{2}-16z-7=0$ can be written in the form $4(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{z}^{2}-16z-7) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{2}+16z+7 & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+16z & =-7 \\\\ 4({z}^{2}+4z) & =-7 \\\\ 4[{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & =-7 \\\\ 4[{(z+2)}^{2}-4] & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2}-16 & =-7 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =-7+16 \\\\ 4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(z+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(z+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z+2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |z+2| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ z & =-2\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =-2-\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ z & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $z=-\\frac{7}{2}$ and $z=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "023e85b9-b324-5fae-8b24-a993cf078c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=6,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269286} {:answer/id #uuid "051cc304-af83-50b4-a01c-91d408eee4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=5,\\,$ $z=2,\\,3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269314} {:answer/id #uuid "9b05c443-66c4-5788-9945-5ae98a6cd27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270638} {:answer/id #uuid "ec254ead-7c72-506d-8d45-a216984be5dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=2,\\,3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271344} {:answer/id #uuid "f2794853-d747-5b28-8e9f-17e9eaced79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=5,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b05c443-66c4-5788-9945-5ae98a6cd27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=9,\\,$ $z=-1,\\,5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270638} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b14b80a-26a2-5d34-a91a-5c29354c2e02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "948cfe82-2b59-583b-87a6-8211c3b5ce23" :question/math-academy-id "q-141627" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-15=12z$ can be written in the form $(z-p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, divide it by the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-15 & =12z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-12z & =15 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-4z) & =15 \\\\ {z}^{2}-4z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z & =5 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot 2\\cdot z+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-2)}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ {(z-2)}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=2$ and $q=9.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{(z-2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ |z-2| & =3 \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =2\\pm 3 \\\\ z & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506516 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ab5c423-17d6-5038-a462-ea15f5b2e87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506183} {:answer/id #uuid "a9a5c631-15ae-5872-945b-175674fabfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506221} {:answer/id #uuid "bd51cecd-6c04-520f-9fa4-54a69b142f90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506252} {:answer/id #uuid "cb0938db-92c7-585b-8cbd-31116bc33d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506271} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5e520c-9345-5603-92aa-5989d3118aeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ab5c423-17d6-5038-a462-ea15f5b2e87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506183} :answer-field/id #uuid "e38d1eab-b846-57b1-8eca-3e79f6301a02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1724c4ea-89ed-51e2-a2bf-8d02f0f074fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-141806" :question/problem "Solve the equation $4{x}^{2}-8x=5.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-8x & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =5 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =5 \\\\ 4[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $4$ to both sides and then dividing by $4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-1)^{2}-4 & =5 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2}-4+4 & =5+4 \\\\ 4(x-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ba4407-03eb-51b1-b004-b940f852c317" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269265} {:answer/id #uuid "03a57f9c-3706-5c7b-9183-93bf72cd124d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269301} {:answer/id #uuid "3a359fbb-bf86-5e11-909d-f780a241551a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269799} {:answer/id #uuid "ad957086-768b-5537-8625-1aa43a2bf2bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-4\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270814} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0efdd1-5309-518f-b349-e728470eb25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03a57f9c-3706-5c7b-9183-93bf72cd124d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269301} :answer-field/id #uuid "f092d464-9988-5471-9683-fb59377b140b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03b9f129-6f00-5dcf-9f10-c4784f08b856" :question/math-academy-id "q-141824" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-6{y}^{2}+24y=21.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}-4y+4$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4y+4=(y-2)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{y}^{2}+24y & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-4y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y) & =21 \\\\ -6({y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}) & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}] & =21 \\\\ -6[(y-2)^{2}-4] & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+(-6)(-4) & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $24$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-6),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(y-2)^{2}+24 & =21 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2}+24-24 & =21-24 \\\\ -6(y-2)^{2} & =-3 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-6)} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y-2)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\ |y-2| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290c2ed7-3b4a-5f80-9ab8-6d2264b502c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6\\pm \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269637} {:answer/id #uuid "5afc9e05-2f19-56d4-8fd8-4dd154cab168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-6\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270079} {:answer/id #uuid "911deb27-d995-5dea-8e24-824959937b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270530} {:answer/id #uuid "b53b11d7-ba13-5f62-b662-6e3b9d578056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{7}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270880} {:answer/id #uuid "d400bdca-5156-56ca-8976-ce67bf79adee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d400bdca-5156-56ca-8976-ce67bf79adee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271133} :answer-field/id #uuid "44e5bd75-2087-50a4-836f-a79a60347260" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2ec4003-7bb2-5f7b-b2d0-ca23e28bb9c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-141826" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-7{y}^{2}-42y=62.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${y}^{2}+6y+9$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+6y+9=(y+3)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{y}^{2}-42y & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+6y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y) & =62 \\\\ -7({y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-{3}^{2}] & =62 \\\\ -7[(y+3)^{2}-9] & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+(-7)(-9) & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $63$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-7),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(y+3)^{2}+63 & =62 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2}+63-63 & =62-63 \\\\ -7(y+3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{(-1)}{(-7)} \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ \\sqrt{(y+3)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{7}} \\\\ |y+3| & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y+3 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\ y & =-3\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139d882d-3cd8-5591-95ab-4f02b364c9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269444} {:answer/id #uuid "27b5c406-c7ab-5ca1-8538-f5588287699a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{-2\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269620} {:answer/id #uuid "412dd5fe-943d-5e9f-9270-682fb4738770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} {:answer/id #uuid "91c7e265-47ef-51f2-a502-687b1a4fa602" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270539} {:answer/id #uuid "f39b5916-e78a-5378-beb3-0cc0c23fcae0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pm \\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412dd5fe-943d-5e9f-9270-682fb4738770" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269859} :answer-field/id #uuid "05523259-00ab-5145-bcab-f2aec15fcd8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1044f32f-7390-525d-94d0-30c2ecd3e1b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141829" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-4{x}^{2}+4x=-2.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4}={(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}+4x & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x) & =-2 \\\\ -4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}) & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-2 \\\\ -4[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(-4)(-\\frac{1}{4}) & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by subtracting $1$ from both sides and then dividing by $(-4),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1 & =-2 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+1-1 & =-2-1 \\\\ -4{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\ |x-\\frac{1}{2}| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1\\pm \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184232 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c872e855-895e-5369-8a79-cbdf21133548" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dc7b935-bf72-53f8-affa-266bf6c23916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9dc7b935-bf72-53f8-affa-266bf6c23916" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506187} :answer-field/id #uuid "63bb1ee1-44d2-5a8b-a0ff-8a61b2d8e549" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506423} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4b1d8eb-b6f3-5cda-88d1-2fdad4eeb482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b1d8eb-b6f3-5cda-88d1-2fdad4eeb482" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506211} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd21be66-27be-55af-af71-359acfb6c6dc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506507}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cca814ca-cf90-5add-8be7-53a49b278718" :question/math-academy-id "q-218766" :question/problem "Solve the equation $9{x}^{2}-18x=7.$  Write the solutions in ascending order.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-2x+1$ is a perfect square.*\n\n$\\,{x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}} $,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+1=(x-1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}-18x & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x) & =7 \\\\ 9({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1y+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}) & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}] & =7 \\\\ 9[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the perfect square by adding $9$ to both sides and then dividing by $9$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9(x-1)^{2}-9 & =7 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2}-9+9 & =7+9 \\\\ 9(x-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{16}{9} \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506549 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82580df0-052c-5542-a737-2921e6a984bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82580df0-052c-5542-a737-2921e6a984bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506135} :answer-field/id #uuid "6292167a-5bc6-5c4a-8390-f09eba2cfd71" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506419} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079234a2-8e4a-5862-b049-dbc5bc0a3653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505930}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "079234a2-8e4a-5862-b049-dbc5bc0a3653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505930} :answer-field/id #uuid "f81580ec-b17d-59a4-8b79-db833bfb273c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f9c9fb5-398b-5a72-983c-e3a77bff1e92" :question/math-academy-id "q-218773" :question/problem "By completing the square, the equation $3{x}^{2}+12x+8=0$ is equivalent to\n\n$\\,\\,3(x\\,\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+12x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12x & =-8 \\\\ 3({x}^{2}+4x) & =-8 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & -8 \\\\ 3[{(x+2)}^{2}-4] & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(x+2)}^{2}-3\\cdot 4 & =-8 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2}-12 & =-8 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2} & =-8+12 \\\\ 3{(x+2)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290361 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fa7313a-7406-5abc-80c9-a30b143bd862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fa7313a-7406-5abc-80c9-a30b143bd862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288583} :answer-field/id #uuid "755bfc03-bd02-52ab-8aab-d56ea4d74101" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65b6fcfc-869c-5714-9359-faf7c265b47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65b6fcfc-869c-5714-9359-faf7c265b47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288748} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bf4fcaa-8406-516c-8a6c-82c8165c0bcf" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290037} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33ef5193-2ee7-5e06-8117-a25f40f24818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ef5193-2ee7-5e06-8117-a25f40f24818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288375} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c37bd49-077a-53a1-a295-f2ea33481465" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290039} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbf52275-b8bd-52b3-9c51-96b483c07a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289272}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbf52275-b8bd-52b3-9c51-96b483c07a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289272} :answer-field/id #uuid "e031303d-1afc-5f05-b049-a9f58c47689f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94af16d4-6673-5335-b83a-58895c41f826" :question/math-academy-id "q-218774" :question/problem "$$\n-4{x}^{2}+16x-11=0\n$$\n\nBy completing the square, the above equation is equivalent to$\\,\\,4(x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}, and the solutions are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}+16x-11) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-16x+11 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-16x+11 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-16x & =-11 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}-4x) & =-11 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{(2)}^{2}-{(2)}^{2}] & =-11 \\\\ 4[{(x-2)}^{2}-4] & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2}-4\\cdot 4 & =-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2}-16 & =-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & =16-11 \\\\ 4{(x-2)}^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x-2)}^{2} & =5 \\\\ {(x-2)}^{2} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-2)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{4}} \\\\ |x-2| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ |x-2| & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} \\\\ x & =2\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fc9c0e4-94e6-5a58-b402-1569ccdf0546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506114} {:answer/id #uuid "854c086c-10d6-59fc-a728-734ed635b82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=-\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506144} {:answer/id #uuid "9dedfe61-c595-5e0a-ab8d-634757cce0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{25}{4},\\,z=-4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506189} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b96d21-d357-5674-892a-42feaf9e8b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{3}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-7$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506208} {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0eb231a-0f38-50bf-8cf9-3768fa9ab226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p+q=\\frac{19}{4},\\,z=4$ and $z=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506356} :answer-field/id #uuid "9418bd8b-7dee-502f-b277-9872940fd25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2236615a-502f-5e6f-8a3b-d0bb2e05f4c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-35049" :question/problem "The equation $3{z}^{2}-12=9z$ can be written in the form $(z+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}-12 & =9z \\\\ 3{z}^{2}-9z-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({z}^{2}-3z-4) & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-3z-4 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} & =4 \\\\ {z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ [{z}^{2}-2\\cdot z\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}]-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-\\frac{3}{2},q=\\frac{25}{4}$ and $p+q=\\frac{19}{4}.$\n\nNow, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(z-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |z-\\frac{3}{2}| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z-\\frac{3}{2} & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}=4 \\\\ z & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{5}{2}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=4$ and $z=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ef3962e-a6bb-5a67-a558-58cd427de315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505964} {:answer/id #uuid "33d18b14-4374-57fc-bccd-ad6999325131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506002} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d4b756-0892-5fb7-92ca-8db3da31c1e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506240} {:answer/id #uuid "d75d4ab0-4dfc-5974-92a8-1f8911001872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506297} {:answer/id #uuid "eb226441-7167-55d6-90f6-42c6f33cce44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506341}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d75d4ab0-4dfc-5974-92a8-1f8911001872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q-p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506297} :answer-field/id #uuid "6773a66b-b95a-576d-aff0-70a25d61a27e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183487}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d367cd34-05c6-5d03-a048-21bce9745fe2" :question/math-academy-id "q-60351" :question/problem "The equation $5{x}^{2}+2x-3=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q-p.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}x) & =3 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}x+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}] & =3 \\\\ 5[{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}] & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{25} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5} & =3 \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =3+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 5{(x+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $q=\\frac{16}{5}.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nq-p=\\frac{16}{5}-\\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{15}{5}=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30938dcd-6958-5d27-99ab-ac151ecf0f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505998} {:answer/id #uuid "51247d6a-6677-500b-b718-1c064593b669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506048} {:answer/id #uuid "83912612-984f-5701-9ae3-68c22057a433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506141} {:answer/id #uuid "b34d4a38-3be9-5e99-9a2a-f46dac84f6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506239} {:answer/id #uuid "bca3e5a5-72f7-53a4-98d8-5a74a18fa611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b34d4a38-3be9-5e99-9a2a-f46dac84f6db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506239} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a373b5f-ae93-59e6-ae47-3ffe140f606a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "219e2398-5583-50ea-9262-64c35b6b5639" :question/math-academy-id "q-60357" :question/problem "The equation $-5{x}^{2}+5x-1=0$ can be written in the form $5(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-5{x}^{2}+5x-1) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-5x & =-1 \\\\ 5({x}^{2}-x) & =-1 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}] & =-1 \\\\ 5[{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}] & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4} & =-1 \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =-1+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 5{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $q=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "257b907c-70c1-5bbd-a6d2-297d51325d1f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4729e5c5-d121-5071-90ac-905326cae80d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403087} {:answer/id #uuid "148ab9f1-4a75-549b-80f9-e24ca927be0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=-1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403088} {:answer/id #uuid "a69056b8-e131-5a3d-920b-99466a245297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403089} {:answer/id #uuid "0a32de8d-323c-57e3-a4c6-10f2b23289bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403090} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a7de99-c75b-55c2-bfe2-679645b43eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=-1,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69056b8-e131-5a3d-920b-99466a245297" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "q=11,\\,x=1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403089} :answer-field/id #uuid "08090f79-b14c-5148-89c6-fad864772e14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403086}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "404aac0a-c4f3-5720-8a82-6f8114d49cae" :question/math-academy-id "q-60360" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}-4x-9=0$ can be written in the form $2(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-4x-9 & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-4x & =9 \\\\ 2({x}^{2}-2x) & =9 \\\\ 2[{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot x+{(1)}^{2}-{(1)}^{2}] & =9 \\\\ 2[{(x-1)}^{2}-1] & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2}-2 & =9 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =9+2 \\\\ 2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(x-1)}^{2} & =11 \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x-1)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ |x-1| & =\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\ x & =1\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d50330c-784c-5a84-ac1e-5c7c5be92530" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ and $x=\\frac{13}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505935} {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} {:answer/id #uuid "8910933f-a80b-5b82-961d-2627b5f5280c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506150} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ee5ca9-ea89-54cd-839a-29262cd0a71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{2},\\,x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506213} {:answer/id #uuid "f62dae0d-aa09-5a1e-b79c-fc4344431af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3}{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2393a4d4-762d-5560-9226-5a0e216dec51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$p=\\frac{3}{4},\\,x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505969} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcb7d856-8042-5bbe-be63-7001e1b25a2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506486}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6c6b5462-ae7e-592e-8c21-3452425e7810" :question/math-academy-id "q-60363" :question/problem "The equation $-4{x}^{2}-6x+10=0$ can be written in the form $4(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $p,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{x}^{2}-6x+10) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+6x-10 & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6x & =10 \\\\ 4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x) & =10 \\\\ 4[{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}x+{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}] & =10 \\\\ 4[{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{16}] & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{9}{16} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4} & =10 \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =10+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{4} \\\\ {(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} & =\\frac{49}{16} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(x+\\frac{3}{4})}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{16}} \\\\ |x+\\frac{3}{4}| & =\\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\pm \\frac{7}{4} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\pm \\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}=-\\frac{10}{4}=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{7}{4}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3996c79f-9281-5b9d-9b33-8863f10adc33" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} {:answer/id #uuid "fff341dd-b41a-5304-ad09-cc4af765adfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404346} {:answer/id #uuid "0b80197a-b970-5c44-9c91-75cf359e8575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=5$ and $w=\\frac{11}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404347} {:answer/id #uuid "fe959228-e459-5b19-9cf7-7d8a7111d3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=15,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404348} {:answer/id #uuid "89145d06-76ec-5ca5-93f9-df5b33533d95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}$ and $w=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c07dfad2-047a-527a-a51a-4f998f1949f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$q=25,\\,w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404345} :answer-field/id #uuid "417df0a0-d7e8-5502-826d-e0777e84a95f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "211d779e-92bf-5786-beb2-56b9bec0ebf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86415" :question/problem "The equation $-4{w}^{2}+40w-75=0$ can be written in the form $4(w+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)\\cdot (-4{w}^{2}+40w-75) & =(-1)\\cdot 0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we move the constant term to the right-hand side, factor the coefficient of the quadratic term, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{w}^{2}-40w+75 & =0 \\\\ 4{w}^{2}-40w & =-75 \\\\ 4({w}^{2}-10w) & =-75 \\\\ 4[{w}^{2}-2\\cdot 5w+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2}] & =-75 \\\\ 4[{(w-5)}^{2}-25] & =-75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we expand the brackets, and write the equation in the required form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2}-4\\cdot 25 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2}-100 & =-75 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =-75+100 \\\\ 4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=25.$ We now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{(w-5)}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {(w-5)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\sqrt{{(w-5)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\ |w-5| & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w-5 & =\\pm \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ w & =5\\pm \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =5+\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{2} \\\\ w & =5-\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $w=\\frac{5}{2}$ and $w=\\frac{15}{2}.$"}]}
